  "id": "COMPOUND:41490 - PROTEIN:4886",
  "verbalization": "Trans-Myrtanol is implicated in the regulation of Serine/threonine-protein kinase ATR through a series of molecular interactions. The evidence suggests that trans-Myrtanol acts as a target of multiple compounds, including D-Tryptophan and Eupatoroxin, which in turn target ATR. This indicates that trans-Myrtanol may exert its effects through a shared mechanism of action, possibly by modulating the activity of the Splicing factor, proline- and glutamine-rich protein, which is a common target of these compounds. The presence of a direct interaction between trans-Myrtanol and ATR, as inferred from the path_139688, adds a layer of specificity to this relationship. The scores from the paths suggest a consistent and plausible pathway, though the exact mechanism by which trans-Myrtanol influences ATR remains to be fully elucidated. The integration of these findings supports the hypothesis that trans-Myrtanol may function through a mechanism involving the regulation of splicing factors, which in turn affect ATR activity.",
